# Life History and Scaling
#
# Why organisms grow, breed, and age on the schedules they do: trade-offs that replaced the r and K scheme, allometry, and the contested exponent of metabolic scaling.

#>development_ecology_life_history
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!development_ecology
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Life History and Scaling"
 ~#claim: "Life histories are built from trade-offs between growing, breeding, and surviving, and body size scales metabolism by an exponent that is still argued."
 ~#proof: "Trade-off theory from Stearns 1992 and Reznick and colleagues 2002; allometry from Huxley 1932; the three-quarter exponent from Kleiber, the two-thirds case from White and Seymour 2003, and the variation from Glazier 2005."
 ~#door: "Read the r and K story as history, then the trade-offs, then the scaling dispute."
 ~#audience: "advanced students of ecology and evolution"
 continuity: "Life History and Scaling | trade-offs"
 cold_open: "Every life spends a fixed budget on growing, breeding, and surviving, and the trade-offs set its pace."
}

